WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.420
The first film showed that what you choose first sets what genetics can find.

2
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.860
If Cas9 cuts the chosen gene, have you made a knockout?

3
00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:16.850
A blue guide carries gold Cas9 to a matching purple gene region.

4
00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:21.550
A nearby orange PAM licenses binding and cleavage.

5
00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:27.330
Without a PAM beside it, a fully matching site is effectively ignored.

6
00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:32.310
With it, the complex makes a double-strand break at the chosen site.

7
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.110
The cut is only a handoff.

8
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.930
One repair route rejoins ends without a supplied template.

9
00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:44.750
It may restore the sequence or leave an insertion or deletion.

10
00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.430
Another route copies a supplied template.

11
00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:53.570
One programmed site can therefore produce several repaired alleles.

12
00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:58.370
Their frequencies depend on local sequence and cell context.

13
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.900
Follow an allele card through each checkpoint.

14
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.240
Targeting means Cas9 reached the intended site.

15
00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.000
A cut means DNA broke.

16
00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.600
An edited allele carries a sequence change after repair.

17
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.400
A functional knockout has lost gene function.

18
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.760
These states require different evidence.

19
00:01:21.783 --> 00:01:24.923
Now the dish contains several repaired allele cards.

20
00:01:26.203 --> 00:01:28.783
Some are unchanged, some shortened, and some lengthened.

21
00:01:30.103 --> 00:01:32.883
Must every cell in this dish have lost gene function?

22
00:01:34.263 --> 00:01:35.763
Look closely before you decide.

23
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:48.260
No. Cells can carry different pairs of alleles, because a diploid cell carries a

24
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.720
pair. Some pairs contain an unchanged or in-frame allele.

25
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.180
Others carry frameshifts in both copies.

26
00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.620
The dish is a mixed population, and an allele count is not a cell count.

27
00:02:04.383 --> 00:02:08.203
Even frameshifts in both copies are still sequence predictions.

28
00:02:09.503 --> 00:02:13.223
A frameshift always shifts the reading frame downstream of the indel.

29
00:02:14.443 --> 00:02:18.003
Whether the protein's function is gone needs its own measurement.

30
00:02:19.203 --> 00:02:21.963
An in-frame change can retain partial function.

31
00:02:23.423 --> 00:02:26.143
Genotype narrows the claim; it does not finish it.

32
00:02:27.117 --> 00:02:31.177
Derive single-cell clones and identify both alleles separately.

33
00:02:32.597 --> 00:02:36.057
Look beyond a short amplicon when large changes could be missed.

34
00:02:37.277 --> 00:02:39.257
Measure the gene product or activity.

35
00:02:40.617 --> 00:02:43.137
Then ask whether the phenotype follows the genotype.

36
00:02:44.437 --> 00:02:46.057
Each step moves the claim forward.

37
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:51.190
Use independent guides that alter the same gene at different sites.

38
00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:57.030
If they reproduce the phenotype, a guide-specific effect becomes less likely.

39
00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.410
Restore the gene in a way that resists the perturbation.

40
00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:06.250
If the phenotype reverses, the causal link becomes stronger.

41
00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:09.930
Still, no single control proves everything.

42
00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:12.473
Return to the pipeline.

43
00:03:14.013 --> 00:03:17.373
Cas9 can program a cut, but repair determines the allele.

44
00:03:18.833 --> 00:03:23.753
Sequencing can identify that allele, but function needs a phenotype or molecular

45
00:03:23.753 --> 00:03:30.253
readout. When genotype and phenotype agree across independent tests, the claim earns

46
00:03:30.253 --> 00:03:30.733
support.
